  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13928579/assets/b8bbe0874d3fab822db551541916b6792d79399f41ac73f4ce119c9aebb6bc68.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is positive.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is $-1,$ which means that $P(0)=-1.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1.$",
